        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry namest="c1" nameend="c3">Plant identification and flowering period: the common name used
              in most garden centers and nurseries, the main flowering season for plants grown outdoors in
              a temperate climate, and the average height reached by a mature, healthy plant under normal
              growing conditions. Flowering periods can shift by two to four weeks depending on the
              region, the altitude, and the weather of the current year, so use them as an approximate
              guide when planning a garden that should stay in bloom from early spring until late autumn.
              Heights are given for plants grown in open ground; plants grown in containers usually stay
              smaller.</entry>
            <entry namest="c4" nameend="c6">Growing conditions recommended for healthy growth and abundant
              flowering: the amount of direct sunlight the plant needs each day, how often it should be
              watered during the growing season, and the soil type in which it develops best. These values
              describe the ideal conditions; most plants tolerate small variations, but a plant kept for a
              long time in the wrong light, with too much or too little water, or in an unsuitable soil
              will produce fewer flowers and is more exposed to pests and diseases.</entry>
            <entry namest="c7" nameend="c8">Maintenance: how often to apply a balanced fertilizer during the
              growing season, and whether the plant can be grown successfully in pots or containers on a
              balcony or terrace. Stop fertilizing at the end of summer so that the plant can prepare for
              winter, and always follow the dosage recommended by the fertilizer manufacturer.</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>Common name</entry>
            <entry>Flowering season</entry>
            <entry>Average height (cm)</entry>
            <entry>Light exposure</entry>
            <entry>Watering needs</entry>
            <entry>Preferred soil</entry>
            <entry>Fertilizing schedule</entry>
            <entry>Suitable for containers</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>As commonly used in garden centers and nurseries; botanical names are not included in
              this guide</entry>
            <entry>Northern Hemisphere, temperate climate; the main season in which most of the flowers
              open</entry>
            <entry>Measured at full maturity, without supports, for plants grown in open ground</entry>
            <entry>Full sun means at least 6 hours of direct sunlight; shade means less than 3 hours</entry>
            <entry>During the growing season; reduce watering in winter and for plants kept indoors</entry>
            <entry>Well-drained soil is recommended for all plants; avoid soil that stays wet for long</entry>
            <entry>Balanced fertilizer, applied from spring until late summer, never in winter</entry>
            <entry>Minimum pot depth of 30 cm for larger plants; use pots with drainage holes</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>See the plant label for the exact variety</entry>
            <entry>Some varieties flower again in autumn</entry>
            <entry>Dwarf varieties can be much smaller</entry>
            <entry>Protect from strong afternoon sun in summer</entry>
            <entry>Water early in the morning, at the base of the plant</entry>
            <entry>Add compost once a year, in spring</entry>
            <entry>Use half the dose for young plants</entry>
            <entry>Move containers to a sheltered place in winter</entry>
          </row>
